  <section class="hero">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">About the publication</p>
      <h1>About Food Supply Crisis Report</h1>
      <p>Food Supply Crisis Report explains food-supply risks with a focus on evidence, context and practical responses.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <h2>What we cover</h2>
      <p>Our coverage follows the connected systems that influence food availability and affordability: food prices, crop production, food insecurity, climate and weather, water, fertilizer, shipping, trade routes, storage and resilience.</p>
      <p>We publish a mix of explainers, analysis and time-sensitive reporting. Country and regional articles are designed to make clear when a problem is local or regional rather than evidence of a worldwide shortage.</p>

      <h2>How we use evidence</h2>
      <p>Articles prioritize original material from organizations such as FAO, WFP, the Integrated Food Security Phase Classification, national statistical agencies, agriculture ministries, climate agencies and other primary or authoritative sources. Source links are provided so readers can inspect important data, estimates and forecasts themselves.</p>
      <p>We distinguish confirmed observations from projections and scenarios. A forecast is not presented as a fact, and a price increase is not automatically described as a food shortage. Headlines and summaries are intended to describe the evidence rather than amplify fear.</p>

      <h2>Editorial standards</h2>
      <p>Articles are reviewed for attribution, clarity, date context and internal consistency. Material factual errors should be corrected, and important updates may be reflected in an article's updated date. See our <a href="/editorial-policy/">Editorial Policy</a> for the full standard.</p>

      <h2>Corrections and reader input</h2>
      <p>Readers can report a factual error or suggest a stronger source through the <a href="/contact/">Contact</a> page. A useful correction request identifies the article, the specific claim and a reliable source that supports the proposed change.</p>

      <h2>Advertising and editorial independence</h2>
      <p>The site uses Google AdSense on eligible content pages. Advertising is a separate presentation layer and is not used as a substitute for editorial evidence. Legal and utility pages, including this page, are kept free of advertising.</p>

      <h2>Publisher transparency</h2>
      <p>This publication is operated under the name <strong>Food Supply Crisis Report</strong>. Articles are attributed to the Food Supply Crisis Report Editorial Desk, and the site's <a href="/about/editorial-desk/">Editorial Desk</a> page explains our authorship and sourcing approach.</p>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Read the evidence yourself.</strong>
        <p>Every published article includes a Sources &amp; Further Reading section. For the core organizations we monitor regularly, see the <a href="/sources/">Sources</a> page.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
